  body: "# Lapsing an appeal after revision\n\n## Authored interpretation\n\nThe source relates lapsing an appeal
    to the outcome of a revision and expressly says that supersession cannot lapse an appeal.\n\n## Scope and exceptions\n\
    \nThe discretion must not be used to prevent an appeal being heard. Partial revision, contact with the claimant
    or representative, and new dispute rights need review alongside the source’s differing dated routes.\n\n## Inspected
    source passages\n\n- [DMG 06160, PDF page 31](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6a212d4adbcb60fc6ccb2a0c/dmg-vol-1-ch-6.pdf#page=31)
    — DMG Vol 1 Ch 6: Making appeals and staying.\n- [DMG 06161, PDF page 31](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6a212d4adbcb60fc6ccb2a0c/dmg-vol-1-ch-6.pdf#page=31)
    — DMG Vol 1 Ch 6: Making appeals and staying.\n- [DMG 06165, PDF page 33](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6a212d4adbcb60fc6ccb2a0c/dmg-vol-1-ch-6.pdf#page=33)
    — DMG Vol 1 Ch 6: Making appeals and staying.\n\n## Evidence and review status\n\nModel-assisted interpretation
    of the 15 September 2026 source snapshot; unreviewed and requiring specialist review. The source is DWP guidance,
    not a consolidated statement of legislation. This is an independent experimental publication, not an official
    DWP document, an entitlement decision or an executable benefits rule.\n\nPDF page numbers are one-based file
    positions. Machine extraction can alter reading order, footnotes and characters. Quoted relationships preserve
    extracted text; no visual or specialist acceptance is claimed. Source dates, legal effects and current applicability
    remain separate review tasks.\n\n- `dmg-vol1-ch6` PDF SHA-256: `dcd2a7ede4499792d9bc6762c94a2d6c0ca6e811cb077abbbd4e41e4d405a4e4`;
    extracted pages SHA-256: `e95434f7e856adbd1c8550b369066644a6bf6aa7567af05a1d41768d3dae0c3e`.\n\nThe publicly
    acquired chapter 6 PDF carries the printed footer “Official - DWP Use Only”. This observation is preserved alongside
    its public acquisition URL; the source owner should clarify any conflict with intended reuse. It is not evidence
    of access to unpublished material.\n"
  semantic_relations:
  - target: term/dmg-revision
    predicate: http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#related
    rationale: The source makes the revised outcome relevant to lapsing and then qualifies how the revision
      power should be used.
    evidence:
    - page: page/dmg-vol1-ch6/0031
      quote: "06160 Where\n\n\n1. the DM revises the decision under appeal (for decisions notified before 28.10.13)
        or\n\n\n2. the appeal is accepted by HMCTS, the DM can still consider revising the decision under appeal
        (for\ndecisions notified on or after 28.10.13)\n\n\nthe outcome determines whether the appeal lapses. An
        appeal should be lapsed where the revised\n\ndecision is to the claimant’s advantage1.\n\n\nNote: An appeal
        cannot be lapsed where the decision is superseded.\n\n\n                                               \
        \                                           1 SS Act 98, s 9(6)"
      locator: DMG 06160
    - page: page/dmg-vol1-ch6/0031
      quote: "06161 The purpose of lapsing an appeal is to prevent unnecessary appeals going ahead. The power to\n\
        revise is discretionary rather than mandatory, and should not be used in order to prevent an appeal being\n\
        heard. DMs are therefore advised to consider whether a decision under appeal should be revised.\n\n\nYou
        can revise and lapse an appeal where the decision will give the claimant everything they could get\nfrom
        the tribunal. For example:\n\n\n-     with DLA you can award the highest rate of the care and mobility component\n\
        \n\n-     with ESA you can put the claimant in the support group\n\n\nWhere the decision would not give
        the claimant everything as above, you would be making a ‘partial\nrevision’ – see examples below. Before
        you make a partial revision you must contact the claimant and/or\ntheir representative, to discuss the new
        decision. You must refer to the “Best Practice Memorandum”\nwhich is found in your operational instructions."
      locator: DMG 06161
  - target: term/dmg-supersession
    predicate: http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#related
    rationale: 'This is an association by explicit distinction: the source says an appeal cannot be lapsed by supersession.'
    evidence:
